      <span>🎧 Acoustic Building Science</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span>ASTM C645 &amp; GA-216 Standards</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Resilient Channel (RC-1) Soundproofing: Flange Orientation &amp; Screw Rules
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      Resilient channels (RC-1 or RC-Deluxe) are one of the most cost-effective ways to increase a wall's Sound Transmission Class (STC) from STC 34 up to STC 55. But acoustical test laboratories report that over <strong>75% of field-installed resilient channels fail</strong> due to installer errors that short-circuit the mechanical spring! Here is how to install them properly.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Mounting Flange Rule</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">Always Point Flange UP</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Vertical Spacing</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">24" o.c. (16" for Ceilings)</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Base Layer Screw Length</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-red-600 mt-0.5">1-1/4" Max (No Stud Contact)</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">How Resilient Channels Work (The Mechanical Spring)</h2>
    <p>
      Sound travels through standard walls via solid mechanical vibration: sound waves hit Drywall Leaf A, vibrate the wooden wall studs, and vibrate Drywall Leaf B on the other side.
    </p>
    <p>
      A resilient channel is a roll-formed 25-gauge galvanized steel strip with a perforated mounting flange and a flexible, floating face flange. When installed on studs, it acts as a <strong>mechanical shock absorber</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Drywall is screwed strictly to the floating face of the channel.</li>
      <li>When sound waves hit the drywall, the drywall vibrates against the flexible metal spring rather than transferring energy directly into the solid wood studs.</li>
      <li>This decouples the drywall mass from the frame, dramatically cutting sound transmission.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The Flange UP Rule: Gravity Cantilever Physics</h2>
    <p>
      On vertical walls, resilient channels must ALWAYS be hung horizontally with the <strong>fastening flange pointing UPWARD</strong> (with the open channel opening downward):
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-gray-200 my-4 not-prose">
      <div class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-sm mb-2">Why Flange Up is Mandatory:</div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-0">
        When heavy 5/8-inch drywall sheets are hung on the floating channel face, gravity pulls the drywall downward. With the mounting flange on top, the weight of the drywall pulls the floating leg slightly <strong>OUTWARD and AWAY from the wall stud</strong>, preserving the open air spring cavity! If you install the channel with the flange pointing down, the heavy drywall pulls the channel tightly backward against the wood stud, collapsing the spring flat and short-circuiting the acoustic isolation! (The only exception is the lowest floor channel, where the flange points down for floor clearance).
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="Elena Rostova, CBO"
    role="Certified Building Official & Material Takeoff Analyst"
    experience="18+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800"
    initials="ER"
    title="The Two-Layer Drywall Screw Length Rule"
    note="When building a high-performance STC 58 soundproof wall with two layers of 5/8-inch drywall over resilient channels: For the base layer (first sheet of 5/8-inch drywall), use Type S fine-thread 1-1/4 inch drywall screws. A 1-1/4 inch screw passes through 5/8-inch drywall and penetrates the 25-gauge channel face by about 1/2 inch, safely clearing the 3/8-inch airspace behind the channel without touching the wood stud. For the second layer (second sheet of 5/8-inch drywall), do NOT drive longer screws through into the channel! Instead, apply Green Glue damping compound between the sheets, and fasten the second layer directly to the first layer using 1-1/2 inch laminate screws driven into the drywall meat between the channel lines. This completely eliminates the possibility of a long screw hitting a hidden stud and ruining the acoustic decoupling."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Resilient Channel Soundproofing"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Over-Driving Long Screws into Wood Studs",
        costImpact: "$2,400 (Destroys Wall Acoustic Isolation)",
        description: "Driving 2-1/2 inch screws through resilient channels into studs forms a solid steel bridge, collapsing an STC 58 wall down to STC 36.",
        solution: "Use 1-1/4 inch screws for the base layer, and systematically check that the drywall face has a slight flexible spring bounce when pressed with your hand."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Overlapping Channel Ends Rigidly on the Same Stud",
        costImpact: "$650 (Bulging Seams & Acoustic Bridging)",
        description: "Lapping two pieces of resilient channel directly over each other on a single stud creates an inflexible double-thick metal hump that telegraphs through drywall.",
        solution: "Butt channel ends together on the stud face or leave a 1/2-inch gap between channel ends, fastening each side independently."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Fastening Drywall Directly to Top and Bottom Wall Plates",
        costImpact: "$1,100 (Flanking Impact Vibrations Through Frame)",
        description: "Screwing the top or bottom of drywall sheets directly to the framing top plate or bottom sole plate bypasses the resilient channels completely.",
        solution: "Drywall must NEVER be fastened to the top or bottom wood plates; fasten solely to the resilient channels located 2 inches from the ceiling and floor."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Builder Community Reviews -->
  <CommunityReviews
    tradeTitle="Resilient Channel Guide"
    reviews={[
